 ## Background
-3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S1, unofficial]. The initial implementation required significant VRAM for large scenes and suffered from slow convergence due to uniform learning rates and redundant Gaussian computations. Subsequent research focused on optimizing the training loop through parameter-specific learning rates [4276-3d-gaussian-splatting-training-parameter-specific-learning-r.md], memory management via CPU offloading [7351-clm.md], and algorithmic accelerations like FastGS [9177-fastgs.md] and fused kernels [9597-fused-backward-and-optimizer.md]. These methods have become standard in large-scale reconstruction and dynamic scene modeling [S2][S3, unofficial].
